    <figcaption style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;margin-top:8px;">The arrows trace what the engineer carries forward — not a programmatic data pipe between skills. In practice, auth0-management and ha-debug typically run in parallel, since one covers Auth0 events and the other covers AWS-side state.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="scenario">Scenario</h2>
  <p>A LINQ customer support engineer reports a spike of failed logins originating from IP <code>1.2.3.4</code> and says tenant administrator <code>alice@acme-isd.example.com</code> is locked out of the <code>acme-isd</code> district. School district administrators arrive in the morning — the window to resolve this is short. (<code>acme-isd</code> and <code>alice@acme-isd.example.com</code> are fictional; <code>.example.com</code> is an IANA-reserved domain for documentation.)</p>

  <Callout variant="info" label="Read-only by default">
    <p>Four of the five skills in this chain are read-only — auth0-management's logs and sec subcommands, ha-debug's investigation subcommands, and verify-user-authorization. Only the last leg writes: kb-ingest dispatches the knowledge-curator, which is the sole agent permitted to author under <code>knowledge/</code>. verify-user-authorization additionally refuses prod runs without an explicit <code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code> acknowledgment.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="step-1">Step 1 — auth0-management (logs): pull recent events</h2>
  <p>The support engineer opens a conversation with the coordinator and describes what they are seeing: failed logins from an unfamiliar IP. The coordinator dispatches auth0-management's logs subcommand, which translates the request into a Lucene query, computes a literal date range, shows a pre-flight summary by default, and runs only after the engineer confirms.</p>
  <pre><code>/auth0-management logs failed logins from ip 1.2.3.4 in the last 24 hours</code></pre>
  <p>The skill maps "failed logins" to event types <code>fp</code> (wrong password) and <code>fu</code> (invalid username), scopes the query to <code>ip:"1.2.3.4"</code>, and returns a JSON envelope. The fields to notice first are <code>total</code> (how many events matched), <code>logs[].type</code> (the Auth0 event code per attempt), and <code>logs[].user_name</code> (the targeted account). In this scenario the response surfaces twelve events, ten of which name <code>alice@acme-isd.example.com</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "query": "type:(fp OR fu) AND ip:\\"1.2.3.4\\" AND date:[2026-05-05 TO *]",
  "sort": "date:-1",
  "total": 12,
  "fetched": 12,
  "capped": false,
  "logs": [
    { "type": "fp", "ip": "1.2.3.4", "user_name": "alice@acme-isd.example.com" }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
  <p><strong>What the operator now knows:</strong> there are confirmed failure events tied to a real user, <code>alice@acme-isd.example.com</code>. The coordinator's next routing decision — check whether the IP is currently blocked and whether Alice has active Auth0 user-blocks.</p>
  <p><a href={join('/tutorials/auth0-management/')}>Read the auth0-management tutorial →</a></p>

  <h2 id="step-2">Step 2 — auth0-management (sec): inspect the subject</h2>
  <p>The auth0-management sec subcommand answers "what is the current security posture for this IP or user?" — a read-only inspection of the Auth0 anomaly and attack-protection endpoints. There is no pre-flight confirmation step; the subcommand is read-only and the queries are narrow. Run two invocations in sequence: one for the IP, one for the user.</p>
  <pre><code>/auth0-management sec 1.2.3.4</code></pre>
  <p>The skill checks <code>/anomaly/blocks/ips/1.2.3.4</code> and surfaces the <code>block.blocked</code> boolean alongside a recent-activity breakdown by event type (default 7-day window; widen with <code>--days N</code>). If the IP is not yet blocked, the Next Steps suggest running <code>/auth0-management stats failures</code> to check whether it is part of a wider pattern.</p>
  <pre><code>/auth0-management sec alice@acme-isd.example.com</code></pre>
  <p>This invocation checks <code>/user-blocks?identifier=alice@acme-isd.example.com</code>. The response leads with the count of active block records and the most recent timestamp. A non-empty block list confirms Auth0 is enforcing a lockout on Alice's account.</p>
  <p><strong>What the operator now knows:</strong> the IP's block state, and that Alice has active user-blocks. The coordinator's next routing decision — assemble a full case file from the AWS side to see DynamoDB lock state, Cognito status, and CloudWatch errors.</p>
  <p><a href={join('/tutorials/auth0-management/')}>Read the auth0-management tutorial →</a></p>

  <h2 id="step-3">Step 3 — ha-debug: assemble the case file</h2>
  <p>ha-debug covers the AWS and Harmony-Auth side of the investigation — DynamoDB lock state, Cognito user status, and CloudWatch Lambda logs. The <code>assemble-login-failure-case</code> subcommand is the right archetype for this ticket: a user cannot log in and the account may be locked.</p>
  <pre><code>/ha-debug User can't log in, alice@acme-isd.example.com</code></pre>
  <p>The coordinator maps the symptom to <code>assemble-login-failure-case --email alice@acme-isd.example.com --window 24h</code> and queries DynamoDB, Cognito, and CloudWatch in parallel. The three fields that matter most for a lockout are <code>lockState.locked</code> (DynamoDB lock active?), <code>identity.cognitoStatus</code> (is the Cognito account healthy?), and <code>identity.auth0Blocked</code> (is Auth0 enforcing a lockout?). In practice, ha-debug typically runs alongside auth0-management's logs subcommand — together they form one case file. See the <a href={join('/tutorials/ha-debug/')}>ha-debug tutorial</a> for the full field reference.</p>
  <p><strong>What the operator now knows:</strong> whether the lock is at the Auth0 layer, the DynamoDB layer, the Cognito layer, or several at once. The coordinator's next routing decision — confirm whether Alice's DynamoDB authorization record for tenant <code>acme-isd</code> is still active.</p>
  <p><a href={join('/tutorials/ha-debug/')}>Read the ha-debug tutorial →</a></p>

  <h2 id="step-4">Step 4 — verify-user-authorization: confirm tenant access</h2>
  <p>verify-user-authorization reproduces the LINQ ERP <code>HarmonyAuthAuthorize</code> endpoint's decision logic locally, reading DynamoDB directly. It answers definitively whether Alice's records authorize her for the <code>acme-isd</code> tenant — and surfaces any mismatch between the expected and actual record shape.</p>
  <p>The engineer keeps talking to the coordinator in plain English:</p>
  <pre><code>Does alice@acme-isd.example.com have ERP access for tenant acme-isd in dev?</code></pre>
  <p>Behind the scenes, the coordinator dispatches the verify-user-authorization skill, which executes the Python CLI shown on the <a href={join('/tutorials/verify-user-authorization/')}>verify-user-authorization tutorial</a> with the named AWS profile <code>linq-erp-dev</code> and prints an <code>sts:GetCallerIdentity</code> audit banner before any DynamoDB call.</p>
  <p>The <code>authorization.status</code> field in the JSON envelope tells the story. The values most relevant to a lockout investigation are <code>AUTHORIZED_USER</code> (authorization intact), <code>USER_DISABLED</code> (user-in-tenant row inactive — account suspended on the ERP side), <code>TENANT_DISABLED</code> (tenant inactive — overrides all user-side authorization), and <code>TENANT_MISSING_BUT_USER_AUTHORIZED</code> (no tenant row, but user and superuser rows are active — authorized per the C# logic; surface this loudly because it is counterintuitive). Anything other than <code>AUTHORIZED_USER</code> or <code>AUTHORIZED_SUPERUSER</code> is a concrete mismatch the engineer can hand to the team that owns ERP data.</p>
  <p><strong>What the operator now knows:</strong> Auth0 event stream, Auth0 block state, DynamoDB lock state, Cognito status, and the ERP authorization record. The incident is ready to resolve and document.</p>
  <p><a href={join('/tutorials/verify-user-authorization/')}>Read the verify-user-authorization tutorial →</a></p>

  <h2 id="step-5">Step 5 — kb-ingest: close the loop</h2>
  <p>Once the engineer resolves the incident — say, clearing the DynamoDB lock and confirming Alice can log in — the last step is ingesting the resolution write-up into the wiki. The next on-call should not have to retrace this chain from scratch.</p>
  <pre><code>/kb-ingest path/to/resolution-writeup.md</code></pre>
  <p>The skill classifies the reference (an in-repo path), computes a slug and dated filename, proposes a tag set, and dispatches the knowledge-curator to write the raw source, a wiki summary page, and updated index entries under <code>knowledge/wiki/</code>. The curator follows the eight-step ingest workflow from <code>knowledge/SCHEMA.md</code>. After confirmation, the resolution is searchable wiki content — available to every agent and on-call engineer who encounters a similar incident.</p>
  <p><a href={join('/tutorials/kb-ingest/')}>Read the kb-ingest tutorial →</a></p>

  <h2 id="why-system">Why this is a system, not a list</h2>
  <p>Throughout this walkthrough the engineer typed plain English to one agent — the coordinator. They never selected a skill, constructed a Lucene query, invoked a Python script, or looked up a DynamoDB table name. The coordinator interpreted the intent at each step and routed to the right specialist: auth0-management's logs subcommand for the event stream, the sec subcommand for block state, ha-debug for the AWS-side case file, verify-user-authorization for the ERP authorization record, and kb-ingest to close the loop. The engineer's job was to read evidence and make decisions, not to manage tooling.</p>
  <p>Each skill produced structured output — JSON envelopes with named fields — that the next skill consumed without copy-paste or manual reformatting. The <code>user_name</code> from auth0-management's logs subcommand became the subject for the sec subcommand. The <code>lockState.locked</code> from ha-debug corroborated the Auth0 block. The <code>authorization.status</code> from verify-user-authorization confirmed whether the ERP record was consistent. No context was lost between steps; the chain preserved it.</p>
  <p>The final step — kb-ingest — is what distinguishes a closed incident from a system that learns. The resolution write-up becomes a wiki source page, an entity, and a concept entry per Decision 0013's three-layer LLM-wiki pattern. The next on-call who encounters a similar failure at a school district will find that case in the wiki before they run a single command. That compounding return is the point: skills as a system, not a list.</p>
  <p>For the architectural detail behind the coordinator-plus-specialists model, the three-layer wiki, and the skill contract, see <a href={join('/how-it-works/')}>How it works</a>.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
